  "live_records": {
    "q-340851": {
      "dom_id": "step-q340851",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{4}^{2})$ with $P(X\\ge 3)=0.3446,$ find $\\mu .$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu =$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\ge 3) \\\\  & =1-0.3446 \\\\  & =0.6554\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =0.6554 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{3-\\mu }{4}) & =0.6554 \\\\ Φ\\,(\\frac{3-\\mu }{4}) & =0.6554 \\\\ \\frac{3-\\mu }{4} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6554)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have \n\n$$\nΦ(0.40)=0.6554\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6554)=0.40.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3-\\mu }{4} & =0.40 \\\\ 3-\\mu  & =1.60 \\\\ \\mu  & =1.40.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
