    "q-94826": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94826",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{4}^{2})$ with $P(X<2.5)=0.7881,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2.5)=0.7881\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le 2.5)=0.7881\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.5) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.5-\\mu }{4}) & =0.7881 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2.5-\\mu }{4}) & =0.7881 \\\\ \\frac{2.5-\\mu }{4} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.80)=0.7881\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)=0.80.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.5-\\mu }{4} & =0.80 \\\\ 2.5-\\mu  & =3.2 \\\\ \\mu  & =-0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
